WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.234
-Our next guest is a sports
Emmy Award-winning journalist

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:05.033
who hosts "Game Theory
with Bomani Jones."

00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:07.801
New episodes premiere Friday
nights on HBO and HBO Max.

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:10.334
Please welcome to the show
Bomani Jones, everybody.

00:00:10.334 --> 00:00:12.334
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:15.334
[ Rock plays ]

00:00:15.334 --> 00:00:23.868
♪♪

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:26.000
Welcome to the show, Bomani!
I'm so happy to you have here.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.334
-I appreciate it.
You give everybody

00:00:27.334 --> 00:00:28.968
the dap hug like that,
or am I special?

00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:30.901
-You're special. Yeah.
-Okay, just checking.

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:32.434
[ Laughter ]
-You know what?
I don't know if I gave it

00:00:32.434 --> 00:00:34.968
to your sister
when she was here because --

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:37.901
this is very fun --
your sister was the first

00:00:37.901 --> 00:00:39.200
in your family
to be on the show.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.834
She's an author.
She wrote an incredible

00:00:41.834 --> 00:00:43.133
book called
"An American Marriage."

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:45.033
-Yeah. I have to say,
I was very excited

00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:46.601
and proud of myself
until I realized

00:00:46.601 --> 00:00:47.968
I was second place
in my own house

00:00:47.968 --> 00:00:49.200
[ Laughter ]
on being on this
television show, okay?

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.367
-But you do have siblings
who haven't been on the show

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:53.467
as well, right?
-Yeah, I do. I do have
a couple of those, so.

00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:55.467
[ Laughter ]
-Okay, great. So you can
lord it over them.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:57.100
-Yeah. Like my brother lives
across the street from me.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.734
I will definitely have
to tell him that,

00:00:58.734 --> 00:00:59.834
"You're going to have
to figure something out."

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:01.667
He better start telling some
jokes or dancing or something.

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:02.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.968 --> 00:01:04.467
-Now, you have covered sports
for a long time

00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:06.601
-Yes.
-in a lot of different
formats --

00:01:06.601 --> 00:01:09.534
radio, podcasts, on TV --
but now you got your own show.

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:11.968
And with the world of sports
being as big as it is,

00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:13.601
it must be cool
to finally be in a position

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:15.734
to set the agenda every time
you get behind the camera.

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:17.067
-Yeah, like I think when you do

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:18.767
like a radio show
and stuff like that,

00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:21.067
you are in charge,
but it's radio, right?

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:22.868
Like people don't take it nearly
as seriously.

00:01:22.868 --> 00:01:26.000
It's just a little bit of a zoo
of sorts when you do that.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.267
When you get into television,

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:29.767
it's a zillion different people
that are doing this, doing that.

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:31.868
They got all these different
departments to get into.

00:01:31.868 --> 00:01:33.100
And I remember the first day

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.968
that we opened the office up
for "Game Theory,"

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:38.067
we had this meeting and people
were presenting the schematics

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:39.801
for some field piece
that we had.

00:01:39.801 --> 00:01:41.834
And it dawned on me --
I've never been in a meeting

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:43.767
where everybody cares
about what I think

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:45.734
and they're all staring at me
like so intently,

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:47.501
trying to figure out how I feel
about something.

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:49.501
But it's COVID,
so I got a mask on.

00:01:49.501 --> 00:01:50.634
[ Laughter ]
And they just lose it.

00:01:50.634 --> 00:01:52.400
Like they just staring
and they're so scared

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.300
and I got this like giant smile
on my face the whole way

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.534
and they have no idea because
we're trying not to die.

00:01:57.534 --> 00:01:58.834
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.834 --> 00:02:01.267
-That was the right thing
to try not to do.

00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:02.901
One thing you and I have
in common --

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:04.701
I've obviously been doing this
a long time,

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:06.901
you're new at it,
but one thing we agree on --

00:02:06.901 --> 00:02:08.434
we do not care for teleprompter.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:09.667
-I've got a cue card guy.

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:10.934
I've been working on cue cards
my whole career.

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:12.868
But how are you finding
the teleprompter?

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:14.234
Are you getting used to it?
-Yeah, man, I went in

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:15.734
and got a teleprompter coach

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:16.667
-Oh, yeah?
-to help me do that

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:18.400
because like I could read
the teleprompter.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.400
It's just really hard
to be yourself

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.868
while you're reading
the teleprompter.

00:02:21.868 --> 00:02:24.934
Every word that I'd ever said
in public before, really,

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:26.634
was coming
off of the top of my head.

00:02:26.634 --> 00:02:28.534
And, now, all of a sudden
we have to write this down

00:02:28.534 --> 00:02:30.467
and that feels confining
and everything else.

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:32.467
You know, I've got somebody,
he's an actor and he comes in

00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:34.300
and he basically teaches me how
to read again.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.667
[ Laughter ]
-So you feel good?
-I do. I do.

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:37.334
-Alright. I want to ask you
about some of the stuff

00:02:37.334 --> 00:02:38.901
in the world
of sports right now.

00:02:38.901 --> 00:02:41.167
First off, I mean,
it does feel like maybe this

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:42.901
is the third offseason in a row

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:43.767
where this is going
to be a question.

00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:45.534
Tom Brady -- did he come back,
is he going to play

00:02:45.534 --> 00:02:47.200
another year?
What do you think?

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.000
-I feel like,
if you put your whole family

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.801
on the roulette wheel
to go 8-9

00:02:50.801 --> 00:02:53.901
and now, that family isn't there
anymore, why not come back

00:02:53.901 --> 00:02:55.467
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, interesting.
-and play again, right?

00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:57.667
Like, "Sure, I'd love
to go sit in my house by myself

00:02:57.667 --> 00:02:58.901
and eat Hungry Man dinners.

00:02:58.901 --> 00:03:01.067
[ Laughter ]
Yeah. Who doesn't want
to do that?"

00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:04.634
No, I mean, in fairness to him,
any football player I talked to,

00:03:04.634 --> 00:03:07.067
the adjustment
of no longer playing anymore

00:03:07.067 --> 00:03:08.868
is really, really
difficult for them.

00:03:08.868 --> 00:03:10.901
And people will let Tom Brady
play football

00:03:10.901 --> 00:03:12.534
as long as he wants
to play football.

00:03:12.534 --> 00:03:13.868
I, personally, would not want

00:03:13.868 --> 00:03:16.133
to get hit in the kidneys
at age 46,

00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:17.467
but I got different kidneys
than he does.

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:18.801
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:03:18.801 --> 00:03:19.834
There's like less room

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:23.167
between the outside of you
and your kidney.

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:25.634
-There's less room between the
outside of me and my kidneys

00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:27.901
than probably anybody else
on planet Earth.

00:03:27.901 --> 00:03:30.367
[ Laughter ]
I've made peace with that.

00:03:30.367 --> 00:03:32.634
-A cool story has been
Brock Purdy,

00:03:32.634 --> 00:03:34.834
last player drafted
in the NFL draft last year.

00:03:34.834 --> 00:03:36.767
Mr. Irrelevant is the name
they give to that

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:39.367
and now, he's got --
the San Francisco 49ers

00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:41.634
in the championship game.
I feel like you keep waiting

00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:44.801
for it to strike midnight
and him to turn back

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:46.701
into, you know,
regular old Cinderella,

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:48.501
but what's going on here?
-I do hope that he has a measure

00:03:48.501 --> 00:03:49.534
of perspective about this.

00:03:49.534 --> 00:03:52.400
Like you mentioned me being
an Emmy Award winner.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.133
I didn't win an Emmy.

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.334
I got an Emmy.

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:56.901
-[ Laughs ]
-I was an executive producer

00:03:56.901 --> 00:04:00.167
on a "30 for 30" documentary
and so, when they do that

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:02.234
and it wins
Best Documentary Series,

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:03.701
everybody gets an Emmy.

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:05.901
They would've won an Emmy,
no matter what I had done,

00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:07.434
and that makes it better.
-Right.

00:04:07.434 --> 00:04:09.000
[ Laughter ]
-Because I just got it, right?

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.968
Like I didn't have to really --
I did something,

00:04:10.968 --> 00:04:12.067
but I just got it.

00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:13.968
And I hope that Brock Purdy
has the proper perspective

00:04:13.968 --> 00:04:16.601
to understand that,
while he is doing his job,

00:04:16.601 --> 00:04:18.701
if they win a Super Bowl,
he gonna get one.

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:20.834
Like we don't have to make it
more than it actually is.

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:22.367
We can appreciate that.
"Wow, you showed up

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:24.200
for work late
and, the next thing you know,

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.133
everything went well
and you got a win?

00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:27.734
Great for you!" right?

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:31.067
[ Laughter ]
But you gonna get that Emmy.

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:35.434
-You had a great piece
about how crypto became

00:04:35.434 --> 00:04:37.601
very tied in
with professional sports,

00:04:37.601 --> 00:04:39.901
up to and including
the Miami Heat had

00:04:39.901 --> 00:04:42.033
an FTX Arena that they were
playing in.

00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:44.734
Obviously, they're no longer
playing in an FTX Arena,

00:04:44.734 --> 00:04:47.100
due to everything
that happened with FTX.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.367
[ Laughter ]
What was your original --

00:04:48.367 --> 00:04:50.834
Obviously, I think you were
very early here in saying,

00:04:50.834 --> 00:04:52.567
"Hey, this doesn't quite
smell right.

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:54.901
The whole world of crypto
and how quickly sports

00:04:54.901 --> 00:04:56.267
is wanting to have
a partnership with it."

00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:58.701
-Well, I had started looking
into it about ten years ago.

00:04:58.701 --> 00:05:00.901
There was this website
called Silk Road

00:05:00.901 --> 00:05:04.934
that was basically an open air
drug market on the dark web.

00:05:04.934 --> 00:05:07.534
But real drug dealers
don't sell drugs on the Web,

00:05:07.534 --> 00:05:09.234
so you knew it wasn't
real drug dealers,

00:05:09.234 --> 00:05:11.000
but they were making
all their trades with Bitcoin

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.400
and then the FBI showed up

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.300
and they had all these not
actual drug dealers panicked.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.400
And I was like, "Well, tell me
more about this Bitcoin."

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.801
And then I raised the question,
"Why would I trade my money

00:05:19.801 --> 00:05:21.567
for money that's not money,"
right?

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:23.100
[ Laughter ]
Like when I go
out of the country,

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.467
I take my money
and I trade it for other money.

00:05:26.467 --> 00:05:28.200
You're telling me
that I got this money

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.834
that works perfectly well

00:05:29.834 --> 00:05:31.968
and I'm going to trade this
for some other money

00:05:31.968 --> 00:05:33.834
because this other money
might be worth more.

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:36.300
Well, what's going to make the
value of that other money go up?

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.000
"I'm going to tweet about it,"
right?

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.200
[ Laughter ]
And we're just going
to make the value

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.467
of this other money come up.

00:05:41.467 --> 00:05:42.667
And that was just like --

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:44.701
I hear about like the digital
dollar and everything

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:46.601
and I've studied economics
pretty decently and I was like,

00:05:46.601 --> 00:05:48.701
"My dollar look pretty damn
digital right now."

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:50.767
[ Laughter ]
Like I got --

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:52.901
the money in my pocket
is not all the money I have.

00:05:52.901 --> 00:05:54.667
The rest of it is ones
and zeros.

00:05:54.667 --> 00:05:56.000
And I just couldn't see
how other people

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.634
didn't understand the idea

00:05:57.634 --> 00:06:00.634
of turning your real money
into funny money

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:02.100
[ Laughter ]
doesn't add up.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.601
-And you sort of saw this
very clearly

00:06:04.601 --> 00:06:05.801
and I should give you credit.

00:06:05.801 --> 00:06:06.968
A lot of people didn't.

00:06:06.968 --> 00:06:09.234
In the Super Bowl ads
because there were all these

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:11.501
crypto ads last year
in the Super Bowl

00:06:11.501 --> 00:06:12.968
and they had
a very different message

00:06:12.968 --> 00:06:16.000
than sort of the more classic
financial services companies

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.934
that have Super Bowl ads.
-Yeah, no, you see a commercial

00:06:17.934 --> 00:06:20.601
for Merrill Lynch
or for Charles Schwab

00:06:20.601 --> 00:06:22.067
or any of them other dorks that

00:06:22.067 --> 00:06:24.100
[ Laughter ]
get up there and do the trading

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.767
and they're always telling you,

00:06:25.767 --> 00:06:27.067
[ Baritone ]
"Take care of your future."

00:06:27.067 --> 00:06:29.868
It's always very serene
and peaceful.

00:06:29.868 --> 00:06:33.334
These crypto ads were like,
"Fortune favors the brave!"

00:06:33.334 --> 00:06:35.834
Why do I need to be brave
about my money?

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:39.701
[ Laughter ]
My entire life is about not
being brave about my money.

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:41.934
The whole reason
that you take your money

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:44.767
to somebody to invest it is
because you're not brave!

00:06:44.767 --> 00:06:47.701
[ Laughter ]
It is because you are terrified
of a zillion different factors

00:06:47.701 --> 00:06:49.601
and the only people
who are trying to be brave

00:06:49.601 --> 00:06:50.901
with their money are broke!

00:06:50.901 --> 00:06:53.501
[ Laughter ]
So, I understood why,
if you were broke

00:06:53.501 --> 00:06:55.767
and somebody tells you
you can turn five into ten,

00:06:55.767 --> 00:06:57.067
you'll give it a try.

00:06:57.067 --> 00:07:00.334
But, as our good friend
Mr. Madoff showed us,

00:07:00.334 --> 00:07:02.868
[ Laughter ]
once you hear about a certain
level of return,

00:07:02.868 --> 00:07:05.667
if it were real,
everybody would do it.

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:07.767
Like you think about this --
when has there ever been a time

00:07:07.767 --> 00:07:10.133
that somebody has been making
a lot of money off an investment

00:07:10.133 --> 00:07:11.467
and they made commercials
about it?

00:07:11.467 --> 00:07:13.801
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.801 --> 00:07:15.067
It doesn't work that way!

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:16.901
Nobody cares
that much about you!

00:07:16.901 --> 00:07:20.000
[ Laughter ]
-You mean, once they're telling
me, that proves it's not real?

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.501
-That's all I'm saying.
When they are coming

00:07:21.501 --> 00:07:23.667
to offer this to you like,
"Hey, guys, here's a way

00:07:23.667 --> 00:07:26.400
that everybody can get rich,"
no one has ever

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.534
[ Laughter ]
positioned anything to tell us
how we all can get rich.

00:07:30.534 --> 00:07:32.534
It doesn't go that way.
-Well, I still think

00:07:32.534 --> 00:07:34.200
it's pretty good and I'm putting
all my money into it.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:35.467
[ Laughter ]

00:07:35.467 --> 00:07:36.734
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:38.133
-Hey, man, I appreciate it.
-I really appreciate it.

00:07:38.133 --> 00:07:39.467
[ Cheering and applause ]
Bomani Jones, everybody.

00:07:39.467 --> 00:07:40.934
New episodes of "Game Theory"

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:43.200
premiere Friday nights
on HBO and HBO Max.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.133
We'll be right back
with Sam Morrison.

